|\n| [Traded as](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ticker_symbol \"Ticker symbol\") | * [Nasdaq](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nasdaq \"Nasdaq\"): [FTNT](https://www.nasdaq.com/market-activity/stocks/ftnt) * [Nasdaq-100](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nasdaq-100 \"Nasdaq-100\") component * [S&P 500](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/S&P_500 \"S&P 500\") component |\n| Industry | * [Cloud Security](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cloud_Security \"Cloud Security\") * [Cybersecurity](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cybersecurity \"Cybersecurity\") * [Network Security](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Network_Security \"Network Security\") |\n| Founded | 2000; 26 years ago (2000) |\n| Founders | * [Ken Xie](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ken_Xie \"Ken Xie\") * Michael Xie |\n| Headquarters | [Sunnyvale, California](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sunnyvale,_California \"Sunnyvale, California\") , U.S. |\n| Key people | * Ken Xie ([CEO](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chief_executive_officer \"Chief executive officer\")) * Michael Xie ([CTO](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chief_technology_officer \"Chief technology officer\")) * Keith Jensen ([CFO](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chief_financial_officer \"Chief financial officer\")) |\n| Products | * Fortinet Security Fabric * FortiGate NGFW * FortiGate enterprise * FortiGate carrier * FortiSwitch * FortiAP * FortiWeb * FortiSIEM * FortiSandbox * FortiClient * FortiManager * FortiAnalyzer * FortiEDR * FortiAuthenticator * FortiDeceptor * FortiNAC * FortiGate VM * FortiPAM * FortiCamera * FortiVoice * FortiMail * FortiSOAR * FortiADC * FortiCASB * FortiDDoS * FortiToken * FortiDLP |\n| Services | * [Next generation firewalls](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Next-Generation_Firewall \"Next-Generation Firewall\") * [Antivirus programs](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Antivirus_programs \"Antivirus programs\") * [Intrusion-prevention system](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Intrusion-prevention_system \"Intrusion-prevention system\") * [Antispyware](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Antispyware \"Antispyware\") * [Antispam](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Antispam \"Antispam\") * [VPN](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/VPN \"VPN\") * [Wireless security](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wireless_security \"Wireless security\") * [Application control](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Application_firewall \"Application firewall\") * [Web filtering](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Content-control_software \"Content-control software\") * [Security information and event management](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Security_information_and_event_management \"Security information and event management\") |\n| Revenue | Increase [US$](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/United_States_dollar \"United States dollar\")6.80 billion (2025)[[1]](#cite_note-10-K_FY2025-1) |\n| [Net income](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Net_income \"Net income\") | Increase US$1.85 billion (2025)[[1]](#cite_note-10-K_FY2025-1) |\n| Number of employees | 14,138 (2024) |\n| Website | [fortinet.com](http://fortinet.com) |\n| **Footnotes** [[2]](#cite_note-annualrecent-2)[[3]](#cite_note-Greene-3) |\n\n**Fortinet, Inc.** is an American [cybersecurity](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cybersecurity \"Cybersecurity\") company headquartered in [Sunnyvale](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sunnyvale,_California \"Sunnyvale, California\"), [California](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/California \"California\"). It develops and sells security products including [firewalls](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Firewall_(computing) \"Firewall (computing)\"), [endpoint security](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Endpoint_security \"Endpoint security\") and [intrusion detection systems](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Intrusion_detection_system \"Intrusion detection system\"). Fortinet has offices in the US, Canada, and UK.[[4]](#cite_note-4)\n\nFounded in 2000 by brothers [Ken Xie](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ken_Xie \"Ken Xie\") and Michael Xie, the company's first and main product was FortiGate, a physical [firewall](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Firewall_(computing) \"Firewall (computing)\"). The company later added [wireless access points](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wireless_access_point \"Wireless access point\"), [sandbox](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sandbox_(computer_security) \"Sandbox (computer security)\") and messaging security. The company [went public](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Initial_public_offering \"Initial public offering\") in November 2009.[[5]](#cite_note-5)\n\n## History\n\n[[edit](/w/index.php?title=Fortinet&action=edit&section=1 \"Edit section: History\")]\n\n### Early history\n\n[[edit](/w/index.php?title=Fortinet&action=edit&section=2 \"Edit section: Early history\")]\n\nIn 2000, [Ken Xie](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ken_Xie \"Ken Xie\") and his brother Michael Xie co-founded Appligation, Inc., which they renamed ApSecure and ultimately Fortinet (from the phrase \"Fortified Networks\").[[6]](#cite_note-one-6) Fortinet introduced its first product, FortiGate, in 2002, followed by anti-spam and anti-virus software.[[7]](#cite_note-seven-7)[[8]](#cite_note-:3-8)\n\nThe company raised $13 million in private funding from 2000 to early 2003.[[7]](#cite_note-seven-7) Fortinet's first channel program was established in October 2003.[[9]](#cite_note-three-9) The company began distributing its products in [Canada](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Canada \"Canada\") in December 2003 and in the [UK](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/United_Kingdom \"United Kingdom\") in February 2004.[[7]](#cite_note-seven-7) By 2004, Fortinet had offices in [Asia](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Asia \"Asia\"), [Europe](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Europe \"Europe\"), and [North America](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/North_America \"North America\").[[10]](#cite_note-nine-10)\n\n### Growth and expansion\n\n[[edit](/w/index.php?title=Fortinet&action=edit&section=3 \"Edit section: Growth and expansion\")]\n\nFortinet became profitable in the third quarter of 2008.[[11]](#cite_note-11) According to market research firm IDC, by November 2009, Fortinet held over 15 percent of the [unified threat management](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Unified_threat_management \"Unified threat management\") market.[[12]](#cite_note-public-12) Also in 2009, *[CRN Magazine](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/CRN_(magazine) \"CRN (magazine)\")*'s survey-based annual report card placed Fortinet first in network security hardware, up from seventh in 2007.[[13]](#cite_note-13)\n\nIn November 2009, Fortinet had an initial public offering, opening on the [NASDAQ Global Market](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/NASDAQ_Global_Market \"NASDAQ Global Market\") under the ticker symbol FTNT.[[14]](#cite_note-14) By the end of the first day of trading, the company had raised $156 million.[[15]](#cite_note-15) The company later became listed on the [NASDAQ Global Select Market](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/NASDAQ_Global_Select_Market \"NASDAQ Global Select Market\"), and became a component of the [NASDAQ-100](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/NASDAQ-100 \"NASDAQ-100\") index.[*[citation needed](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:Citation_needed \"Wikipedia:Citation needed\")*]\n\nBy 2010, Fortinet had $324 million in annual revenues[[7]](#cite_note-seven-7) and held the largest share of the unified threat management market according to IDC.[[16]](#cite_note-:1-16)\n\n### Recent history\n\n[[edit](/w/index.php?title=Fortinet&action=edit&section=4 \"Edit section: Recent history\")]\n\nIn July 2014, Fortinet announced a technical certification program called the Network Security Expert (NSE) program.[[17]](#cite_note-17) In March 2016, Fortinet launched a Network Security Academy to help fill open cybersecurity jobs in the U.S. Fortinet donated equipment and provided information to universities to help train students for jobs in the field.[[18]](#cite_note-18) Also in 2016, Fortinet launched a program called FortiVet to recruit military veterans for cybersecurity jobs.[[19]](#cite_note-19)\n\nIn January 2017, it was announced that Philip Quade, a former member of the [NSA](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/National_Security_Agency \"National Security Agency\"), would become the company's chief information security officer.[[20]](#cite_note-20)\n\nIn 2019, Fortinet's FortiGate SD-WAN and Next Generation Firewall received a \"Recommended\" rating from NSS Labs.[[21]](#cite_note-21)\n\nIn September 2021, Fortinet pledged to train one million people in support of [President Joe Biden](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/President_Joe_Biden \"President Joe Biden\")'s call to action to address the talent shortage in American cybersecurity.[[22]](#cite_note-22)\n\nIn March 2022, Fortinet announced the termination of operations in Russia. The company has stopped all sales, support, and professional services within Russia.[[23]](#cite_note-23)[[24]](#cite_note-24)\n\nThe same year, NetworkWorld reported that Fortinet had introduced [AI](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_intelligence \"Artificial intelligence\") and [ML](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Machine_learning \"Machine learning\")-based security services utilizing telemetry from its network.[[25]](#cite_note-25)\n\nIn June 2025, Fortinet was added to Investor's Business Daily's Big Cap 20 list of top-rated large-cap growth stocks.[[26]](#cite_note-26)\n\n## Acquisitions\n\n[[edit](/w/index.php?title=Fortinet&action=edit&section=5 \"Edit section: Acquisitions\")]\n\n* In 2008, the company acquired the intellectual property of IPLocks, a [database](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Database \"Database\") security and auditing company.[[27]](#cite_note-27)\n* In August 2009, Fortinet acquired the intellectual property and other assets of Woven Systems, an [Ethernet switching](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ethernet_switch \"Ethernet switch\") company.[[28]](#cite_note-28)\n* In December 2012, Fortinet acquired app-hosting service XDN (formerly known as 3Crowd)[[29]](#cite_note-29)\n* In 2013 Fortinet acquired [Coyote Point](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Coyote_Point_Systems \"Coyote Point Systems\"), a developer of hardware [load balancers](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Load_balancing_(computing) \"Load balancing (computing)\").[[30]](#cite_note-30)\n* In 2015 Fortinet acquired Wi-Fi hardware company [Meru Networks](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Meru_Networks \"Meru Networks\").[[31]](#cite_note-31)\n* In June 2016, Fortinet acquired IT security, monitoring, and analytics software vendor AccelOps.[[32]](#cite_note-Kuranda_2016-32)\n* In June 2018, Fortinet acquired Bradford Networks, a maker of access control and [IoT](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Internet_of_things \"Internet of things\") security solutions.[[33]](#cite_note-33)\n* In October 2018, Fortinet acquired ZoneFox, a threat analytics company.[[34]](#cite_note-34)\n* In late 2019, Fortinet acquired enSilo and CyberSponse.[[35]](#cite_note-35)[[36]](#cite_note-36)\n* In 2020, Fortinet acquired [SASE](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Secure_access_service_edge \"Secure access service edge\") cloud provider OPAQ Networks,[[37]](#cite_note-:0-37) and automated incident management company Panopta.[[38]](#cite_note-38)\n* In March 2021, Fortinet acquired cloud and network security firm ShieldX.[[39]](#cite_note-39)\n* In July 2021, Fortinet acquired application security company Sken.Ai to offer continuous application security testing.[[40]](#cite_note-40)\n* In June 2024, Fortinet acquired Lacework, a data-driven cloud security company for an undisclosed amount.[[41]](#cite_note-41)[[42]](#cite_note-42)\n* In August 2024, Fortinet acquired Next DLP, a cloud based DLP provider company for an undisclosed amount.[[43]](#cite_note-43)\n* In 2025, Fortinet acquired Everest, an Indian company focus in WIFI access points for high density environments.[[44]](#cite_note-44)\n\n## Products and research\n\n[[edit](/w/index.php?title=Fortinet&action=edit&section=6 \"Edit section: Products and research\")]\n\n### Products\n\n[[edit](/w/index.php?title=Fortinet&action=edit&section=7 \"Edit section: Products\")]\n\nFortinet released its first product, FortiGate, a firewall, in 2002, followed by anti-spam and anti-virus software.[[7]](#cite_note-seven-7)[[8]](#cite_note-:3-8) As functions like anti-spam were added, they were made available in a single product along with the firewall and other functions.[[16]](#cite_note-:1-16) FortiGate was updated later to use [application-specific integrated circuit](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Application-specific_integrated_circuit \"Application-specific integrated circuit\") (ASIC) architecture.[[45]](#cite_note-45) The company has used ASIC in several of its products, including to support its [SD-WAN](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/SD-WAN \"SD-WAN\") features.[[46]](#cite_note-46)[[47]](#cite_note-47)\n\nInitially, FortiGate was a physical, rack-mounted product but later became available as a virtual appliance that could run on [virtualization](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Virtualization \"Virtualization\") platforms such as [VMware vSphere](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/VMware_vSphere \"VMware vSphere\").[*[citation needed](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:Citation_needed \"Wikipedia:Citation needed\")*]\n\nIn April 2016, Fortinet began building its Security Fabric architecture so multiple network security products could communicate as one platform.[[48]](#cite_note-48) Later that year, the company added [Security Information and Event Management](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Security_Information_and_Event_Management \"Security Information and Event Management\") (SIEM) products.[[49]](#cite_note-Gagliordi_2016-49) In September 2016, the company announced it would integrate the SIEM products with the security systems of other vendors.[[50]](#cite_note-50)\n\nIn 2017, Fortinet announced the addition of switches, access points, analyzers, sandboxes and cloud capabilities to the Security Fabric, in addition to endpoints and firewalls.[[51]](#cite_note-51)[[52]](#cite_note-52) Later in 2017, Fortinet created a standalone subsidiary, Fortinet Federal, to develop cybersecurity products for government agencies.[[53]](#cite_note-53) Fortinet has received security effectiveness certifications through NSS Labs.[[54]](#cite_note-54)\n\n[![](//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/dd/Fortinet_FortiGate_6501F.png/250px-Fortinet_FortiGate_6501F.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail)](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Fortinet_FortiGate_6501F.png)\n\nFortiGate 6501F\n\nIn 2019, Fortinet grew to 21,000 WAN edge customers, according to a Gartner report.[[55]](#cite_note-55)\n\nIn February 2020, Fortinet released FortiAI, a threat-detection program that uses [artificial intelligence](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_intelligence \"Artificial intelligence\").[[56]](#cite_note-56)[[57]](#cite_note-57) In July 2020, Fortinet launched multi-cloud SD-WAN.[[58]](#cite_note-58) That year, BT Security selected Fortinet and other Threat Alliance members as Critical Partners.[[59]](#cite_note-59)\n\n### Vulnerabilities and credential leaks\n\n[[edit](/w/index.php?title=Fortinet&action=edit&section=8 \"Edit section: Vulnerabilities and credential leaks\")]\n\nIn September 2021, almost 500,000 login names and passwords for Fortinet VPN devices were published on a hacking forum. The credentials were allegedly scraped from devices vulnerable to a 2018 exploit (CVE-2018-13379).[[60]](#cite_note-60)\n\nIn January 2025, the credentials and configuration files of over 15,000 FortiGate devices were leaked on a hacking forum.[[61]](#cite_note-61) The data is believed to have been obtained from devices vulnerable to a 2022 exploit (CVE-2022–40684).[[62]](#cite_note-62)\n\nIn November 2025, a critical vulnerability ([CVE-2025-64446](https://nvd.nist.gov/vuln/detail/CVE-2025-64446), 9.1 CVSS) became public affecting FortiWeb Web Application Firewall[[63]](#cite_note-63). The vulnerability allowed attackers to gain full control over the device by exploiting a path traversal vulnerability combined with an authentication bypass[[64]](#cite_note-64).\n\nIn June 2026, security researcher Volodymyr \"Bob\" Diachenko of SecurityDiscovery.com reported that he had gained access to a threat actor's infrastructure containing credentials for about 74,000 internet-facing FortiGate firewalls and VPN gateways across 194 countries, associated with 21,632 domains; the dataset was dubbed \"FortiBleed\".[[65]](#cite_note-ars-fortibleed-65)[[66]](#cite_note-bc-fortibleed-66) According to researchers, a Russian-speaking, financially motivated group sprayed FortiGate login endpoints with password combinations, intercepted SSL VPN authentication hashes and cracked them on a 45-GPU cluster, then used the recovered passwords to move into victims' internal networks, including [Active Directory](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Active_Directory \"Active Directory\") environments.[[65]](#cite_note-ars-fortibleed-65)[[67]](#cite_note-hns-fortibleed-67) Hudson Rock said the affected organizations included large corporations, government agencies and a Turkish [NATO](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/NATO \"NATO\") defense contractor from which classified documents had been exfiltrated.[[65]](#cite_note-ars-fortibleed-65) The cybersecurity firm SOCRadar later raised its estimate to 86,644 affected devices[[68]](#cite_note-68) and linked the operation's infrastructure to the Lynx and INC ransomware groups.[[69]](#cite_note-69) The US [Cybersecurity and Infrastructure Security Agency](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cybersecurity_and_Infrastructure_Security_Agency \"Cybersecurity and Infrastructure Security Agency\"), the UK [National Cyber Security Centre](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/National_Cyber_Security_Centre_(United_Kingdom) \"National Cyber Security Centre (United Kingdom)\") and the [Australian Cyber Security Centre](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Australian_Cyber_Security_Centre \"Australian Cyber Security Centre\") issued advisories urging affected organizations to reset credentials and enable multi-factor authentication.[[70]](#cite_note-70) Fortinet stated that the activity did not involve a new vulnerability, attributing it to the reuse of credentials from earlier incidents and to brute-force attacks against devices with weak passwords and no multi-factor authentication.[[71]](#cite_note-71)\n\n### Cybersecurity research\n\n[[edit](/w/index.php?title=Fortinet&action=edit&section=9 \"Edit section: Cybersecurity research\")]\n\nIn 2005, Fortinet created the FortiGuard Labs internal security research team.[[72]](#cite_note-SearchSecurity_2016-72)\n\nBy 2014, Fortinet had four research and development centers in Asia, as well as others in the US, Canada and [France](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/France \"France\").[[73]](#cite_note-73)\n\nIn March 2014, Fortinet founded the Cyber Threat Alliance (CTA) with [Palo Alto Networks](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Palo_Alto_Networks \"Palo Alto Networks\") in order to share security threat data across vendors.[[74]](#cite_note-74) It was later joined by [McAfee](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/McAfee \"McAfee\") and [Symantec](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/NortonLifeLock \"NortonLifeLock\").[[75]](#cite_note-75) In 2015, the CTA published a white paper on the CryptoWall ransomware, which detailed how attackers obtained $325 million through ransoms paid by victims to regain access to their files.[[76]](#cite_note-76)\n\nIn April 2015, Fortinet provided threat intelligence to [Interpol](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Interpol \"Interpol\") in order to help apprehend the ringleader of several online scams based in [Nigeria](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nigeria \"Nigeria\"). The scams, which resulted in compromise of business emails and CEO fraud, had cost one business over $15 million.[[77]](#cite_note-77) The following year, in March 2016, Fortinet and [Cisco](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cisco_Systems \"Cisco Systems\") joined [NATO](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/NATO \"NATO\") in a data-sharing agreement to improve their information security capabilities.[[78]](#cite_note-78)\n\nFortinet researchers discovered a spyware that scammed victims by impersonating the [IRS](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/IRS \"IRS\").[[79]](#cite_note-79) Also in 2017, researchers helped identify [malware](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Malware \"Malware\"), called Rootnik, and [ransomware](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ransomware \"Ransomware\"), called MacRansom, that targeted [Android](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Android_(operating_system) \"Android (operating system)\") and [MacOS](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/MacOS \"MacOS\") systems respectively.[[80]](#cite_note-80)\n\n## Controversies\n\n[[edit](/w/index.php?title=Fortinet&action=edit&section=10 \"Edit section: Controversies\")]\n\nIn May 2004, [Trend Micro](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Trend_Micro \"Trend Micro\") filed a legal complaint against Fortinet, alleging the FortiGate product line violated their U.S. patent on server-based antivirus technology. Though the [International Trade Commission](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/International_Trade_Commission \"International Trade Commission\") initially ruled against Fortinet,[[81]](#cite_note-81) the Trend Micro patents at the center of the dispute were later declared invalid in 2010.[[82]](#cite_note-82)\n\nIn April 2005, a German court issued a preliminary injunction against Fortinet's UK subsidiary in relation to source code for its [GPL](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/GNU_General_Public_License \"GNU General Public License\")-licensed elements.[[83]](#cite_note-83)[[84]](#cite_note-84) The dispute ended a month later after Fortinet agreed to make the source code available upon request.[[85]](#cite_note-85)\n\nIn October 2005, an [OpenNet](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/OpenNet_Initiative \"OpenNet Initiative\") study suggested that the military government of [Myanmar](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Myanmar \"Myanmar\"), which was under which at the time was subject to U.S. sanctions for human rights violations, had begun using Fortinet's FortiGuard system for internet censorship. Fortinet stated that their products are sold by third-party resellers and that they acknowledged US [embargoes](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Embargo \"Embargo\");[[86]](#cite_note-86) however, their sales director participated in a ceremony to deliver the firewall product to the prime minister.[[87]](#cite_note-87)[[88]](#cite_note-88)\n\nIn September 2019, Fortinet settled a [whistleblower](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Whistleblower \"Whistleblower\") lawsuit regarding what the company has described as an \"isolated incident\" of sales of intentionally mislabeled Chinese-made equipment to U.S. government end users.[[89]](#cite_note-89)[[90]](#cite_note-90)\n\nIn 2023, Fortinet Fortiguard devices were revealed by Microsoft and members of the [Five Eyes](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Five_Eyes \"Five Eyes\") intelligence network to be the subject of a wide-ranging and ongoing cyberattack by a state-sponsored entity in China.[[91]](#cite_note-91)\n\nIn September 2024, reports surfaced of an unknown person going by the nickname \"Fortibitch\" posting to an unnamed [hacking forum](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Crime_forum \"Crime forum\") that they allegedly stole 440 gigabytes of data from Fortinet's [Microsoft SharePoint](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Microsoft_SharePoint \"Microsoft SharePoint\") server.[[92]](#cite_note-bc-breach-92)[[93]](#cite_note-:2-93) Fortinet confirmed the data breach to [Bleeping Computer](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bleeping_Computer \"Bleeping Computer\") and said that the data breach included \"limited data related to a small number of Fortinet customers\".[[92]](#cite_note-bc-breach-92)[[93]](#cite_note-:2-93)\n\n## References\n\n[[edit](/w/index.php?title=Fortinet&action=edit&section=11 \"Edit section: References\")]\n