evenue of $6.80 billion and net income of $1.85 billion, with services making up 67% of sales. In Q2 2026, revenue grew 26% to $2.05 billion, and Fortinet raised 2026 revenue guidance to $8.02-$8.18 billion.\n\n**How Fortinet makes money:** Fortinet makes money in two ways. First, it sells products: FortiGate firewalls (physical appliances and virtual/cloud versions), plus switches, Wi-Fi access points and other secure-networking hardware and software…\n\n[Revenue & employees](/company/fortinet/financials)[CEO history](/company/fortinet/ceo-history)[Founders](/company/fortinet/founders)[Business model](/company/fortinet/business-model)\n\nAnnual Revenue (FY2025)$6.8B\n\nEmployees15,109\n\nCEOKen Xie\n\nFounded2000\n\nIndustryCybersecurity\n\nBy [Swet Parvadiya](/author/swet-parvadiya)·Last reviewed:\n\n[Data sources: annual reports, SEC filings, methodology](/methodology)\n\nIndustry:[Cybersecurity and network security](/industries)\n\n## Fortinet, Inc.key figures\n\nFortinet, Inc. had revenue of $6.8B for fiscal year 2025, up 14.2% from FY2024. Net income was $1.9B, a net margin of 27.3%.\n\nSource:[FORTINET, INC. annual report (Form 10-K, SEC EDGAR, filed 2026-02-25)](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1262039/000126203926000007/) · Last reviewed October 2026\n\nRevenue growth, FY2016 to FY2025\n:   [+20.4% a year](/company/fortinet/financials)\n:   Compound annual rate, $1.3B to $6.8B\n\nPeak revenue year\n:   [FY2025](/company/fortinet/financials)\n:   The latest annual year is the highest in this series\n\nRevenue per employee\n:   $450,036\n:   FY2025 revenue divided by 15,109 employees; headcount may use another date\n\nCurrent chief executive\n:   [Ken Xie](/company/fortinet/ceo-history)\n:   In the role since 2000 (26 years)\n\nDocumented transactions\n:   [12](/company/fortinet/acquisitions)\n:   Between 2012 and 2025\n\nFounded\n:   [2000](/company/fortinet/company-history)\n:   26 years ago\n\nWhere was Fortinet founded?\n:   Fortinet was founded in Sunnyvale, California in 2000.\n\nWhen did Fortinet start?\n:   Fortinet was founded in 2000.\n\nWho founded Fortinet?\n:   Fortinet was founded by Ken Xie and Michael Xie.\n\nHow old is Fortinet?\n:   Fortinet is 26 years old as of 2026.\n\nIn-Depth Analysis\n\n418 words · 3 min read\n\nFortinet was founded in 2000 by two brothers, Ken and Michael Xie. Ken Xie was already a legend in the cybersecurity industry, having previously founded NetScreen (which he later sold to Juniper Networks for $4 billion). While at NetScreen, Xie realized a fundamental flaw in the cybersecurity architecture of the late 1990s: firewalls were too slow. Traditional firewalls ran on standard, off-the-shelf computer processors (like Intel chips). As the internet grew faster and cyberattacks became more complex (requiring the firewall to inspect the data packets), the standard processors choked, creating major bottlenecks that slowed down corporate networks. Fortinet was built to solve the speed problem.\n\n## The ASIC Hardware Advantage\n\nFortinet's defining strategic decision was to reject off-the-shelf processors. Instead, the company spent millions of dollars designing its own proprietary microchips, known as ASICs (Application-Specific Integrated Circuits). These chips were hard-coded to do one thing: process security data at lightning speed. By placing these custom ASICs inside their physical firewall boxes (the FortiGate) Fortinet was able to offer a product that was faster and significantly cheaper than the competition. While competitors like Palo Alto Networks focused on premium, complex enterprise software, Fortinet dominated the \"value\" segment, selling millions of fast, cheap firewalls to small and medium-sized businesses (SMBs) and large retail chains.\n\n## The Convergence of Networking and Security\n\nAs corporate networks expanded (connecting headquarters with thousands of small remote branch offices), the traditional separation between \"networking\" (routers and switches) and \"security\" (firewalls) became inefficient. Fortinet capitalized on this by pioneering SD-WAN (Software-Defined Wide Area Network). Instead of a corporation buying an expensive Cisco router and a separate Fortinet firewall for every single retail store, Fortinet simply built the routing capabilities directly into their firewall box. This consolidation allowed companies to rip out millions of dollars of legacy networking equipment, cementing Fortinet as the central nervous system of their distributed corporate networks.\n\n## The Cloud Transition Challenge\n\nThe primary existential challenge for Fortinet is the strong, global migration to cloud computing. As corporations move their databases from physical servers in their basements to AWS or Azure, the need for a physical firewall box sitting in a server room diminishes. In response, Fortinet is working to \"virtualize\" its technology, deploying its security software directly into the public cloud and pushing \"SASE\" (Secure Access Service Edge), a framework that delivers security entirely from the cloud to remote workers. Its long-term position depends on proving it can dominate the cloud era just as effectively as it dominated the era of the physical hardware box.\n\n### Table of Contents\n\n### Fortinet, Inc.Key Facts\n\n|  |  |\n| --- | --- |\n| Company | Fortinet, Inc. |\n| Annual revenue change | +14.2% (FY2024–FY2025) |\n| Financials | $6.8B (FY2025, last reviewed October 2026) |\n| Market Cap | $127.3B |\n| FY2025 net income | $1.9B |\n| Last reviewed | By Swet Parvadiya, Founder & Editor - October 2026 |\n| Founded | 2000 |\n| Founder(s) | Ken Xie, Michael Xie |\n| CEO | Ken Xie |\n| Headquarters | Sunnyvale, California |\n| Industry | Cybersecurity and network security |\n| Employees | 15,109+ |\n\n## Fortinet Company Profile: Revenue, Business Model & Strategy\n\n\"Fortinet is what happens when a firewall company refuses to stay only a firewall company.\"\n\nFortinet (Nasdaq: FTNT) reported 202